n</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Ozersky</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Pepin</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Rock</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal></etal></person-group><article-title>The male-specific region of the human Y chromosome is a mosaic of discrete sequence classes.</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>2003</year><volume>423</volume><fpage>825</fpage><lpage>837</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12815422</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/nature01722</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B6"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bachtrog</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Charlesworth</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Reduced adaptation of a non-recombining neo-Y chromosome.</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>2002</year><volume>416</volume><fpage>323</fpage><lpage>326</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11907578</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/416323a</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B7"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lucchesi</surname><given-names>JC</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Gene dosage compensation and the evolution of sex chromosomes.</article-title><source>Science</source><year>1978</year><volume>202</volume><fpage>711</fpage><lpage>716</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">715437</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1126/science.715437</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B8"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Steinemann</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Steinemann</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Enigma of Y chromosome degeneration: neo-Y and neo-X chromosomes of <italic>Drosophila miranda </italic>a model for sex chromosome evolution.</article-title><source>Genetica</source><year>1998</year><volume>102-103</volume><fpage>409</fpage><lpage>420</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9720292</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1023/A:1017058119760</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B9"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Waters</surname><given-names>PD</given-names></name><name><surname>Duffy</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Frost</surname><given-names>CJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Delbridge</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name><name><surname>Graves</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The human Y chromosome derives largely from a single autosomal region added to the sex chromosomes 80-130 million years ago.</article-title><source>Cytogenet Cell Genet</source><year>2001</year><volume>92</volume><fpage>74</fpage><lpage>79</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11306800</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1159/000056872</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B10"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bachtrog</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sex chromosome evolution: molecular aspects of Y chromosome degeneration in <italic>Drosophila</italic>.</article-title><source>Genome Res</source><year>2005</year><volume>15</volume><fpage>1393</fpage><lpage>1401</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16169921</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1101/gr.3543605</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bartolom&#x000e9;</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Charlesworth</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Evolution of amino-acid sequences and codon usage on the <italic>Drosophila miranda </italic>neo-sex chromosomes.</article-title><source>Genetics</source><year>2006</year><volume>174</volume><fpage>2033</fpage><lpage>2044</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17028318</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1534/genetics.106.064113</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B12"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yi</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name></person-group><source>Molecular Variation and the Evolution of the neo-sex Chromosomes of Drosophila miranda</source><year>2000</year><publisher-name>Chicago: University of Chicago</publisher-name></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bachtrog</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Protein evolution and codon usage bias on the neo-sex chromosomes of <italic>Drosophila miranda</italic>.</article-title><source>Genetics</source><year>2003</year><volume>165</volume><fpage>1221</fpage><lpage>1232</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14668377</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bachtrog</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Accumulation of <italic>spock </italic>and <italic>worf</italic>, two novel non-LTR retrotransposons on the neo-Y chromosome of <italic>Drosophila miranda</italic>.</article-title><source>Mol Biol Evol</source><year>2003</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>173</fpage><lpage>181</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12598683</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/molbev/msg035</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bachtrog</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Evidence that positive selection drives Y-chromosome degeneration in <italic>Drosophila miranda</italic>.</article-title><source>Nat Genet</source><year>2004</year><volume>36</volume><fpage>518</fpage><lpage>522</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15107853</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/ng1347</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="other"><article-title>Repeatmasker</article-title><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.repeatmasker.org"/></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pritham</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Feschotte</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Kesseli</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>An Ac-like transposable element family with transcriptionally active Y-linked copies in the white campion, <italic>Silene latifolia</italic>.</article-title><source>Genetics</source><year>2003</year><volume>165</volume><fpage>799</fpage><lpage>807</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14573489</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Okada</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Sone</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Fujisawa</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Nakayama</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Takenaka</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Ishizaki</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Kono</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Shimizu-Ueda</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Hanajiri</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Yamato</surname><given-names>KT</given-names></name><name><surname>Fukuzawa</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Brennicke</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Ohyama</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The Y chromosome in the liverwort <italic>Marchantia polymorpha </italic>has accumulated unique repeat sequences harboring a male-specific gene.</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA</source><year>2001</year><volume>98</volume><fpage>9454</fpage><lpage>9459</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11481501</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1073/pnas.171304798</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Liu</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Moore</surname><given-names>PH</given-names></name><name><surname>Ma</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Ackerman</surname><given-names>CM</given-names></name><name><surname>Ragiba</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Yu</surname><given-names>Q</given-names></name><name><surname>Pearl</surname><given-names>HM</given-names></name><name><surname>Kim</surname><given-names>MS</given-names></name><name><surname>Charlton</surname><given-names>JW</given-names></name><name><surname>Stiles</surname><given-names>JI</given-names></name><name><surname>Zee</surname><given-names>FT</given-names></name><name><surname>Paterson</surname><given-names>AH</given-names></name><name><surname>Ming</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>A primitive Y chromosome in papaya marks incipient sex chromosome evolution.</article-title><source>Nature</source><year>2004</year><volume>427</volume><fpage>348</fpage><lpage>352</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14737167</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1038/nature02228</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kondo</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Hornung</surname><given-names>U</given-names></name><name><surname>Nanda</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Imai</surname><g